# The Signals Officer: hat, overcoat, both hands up, entirely unimpressed.
#
# The tutorial has had this character in it from the start, saying INCOMING
# with `pose=hands_up` -- and no body to say it with, so it fell back to
# data/characters/test/. The drawing this rig is measured from is that pose
# exactly, which is why it ended up here rather than becoming a new name in
# the cast: the game was already asking for it.
#
# ── THIS ONE IS MEASURED, WHICH THE OTHERS ARE NOT ──
#
# The Advisor and the Cryptographer were authored by eye against the engine's
# needs. This one was authored off a drawing, with a ruler, and the difference
# it turned up is worth writing down because it applies to the whole cast:
#
#   THE OTHER RIGS' SHOULDERS ARE NARROWER THAN THEIR HEADS.
#
# The Advisor's clavicle is 16 against a head of 40, the Cryptographer's 20
# against 46 -- about 0.4 head-widths out to each shoulder. Measured off the
# drawing the same span is 1.27 head-widths, THREE TIMES further. That single
# number is most of why a skeleton built to the old proportions reads as a
# child's drawing of a person and this one reads as a man in a coat.
#
# Every length below is stated in HEAD DIAMETERS as well as rig units, so the
# character can be rescaled by changing `size` on the head and multiplying
# through, and so the next person can check the arithmetic rather than trust it.
#
#   landmark                             heads    units (head = 46)
#   neck, chin to shoulder line           0.27     12
#   shoulder line to head centre          0.73     34   (neck 12 + head 22)
#   shoulder half-width, spine to joint   1.27     58
#   upper arm, shoulder to elbow          1.10     51
#   forearm, elbow to wrist               1.01     46
#   hand, wrist to fingertip              1.27     58
#   torso, shoulder line to hips          1.65     76
#
# THE HANDS ARE LONGER THAN THE FOREARMS, which is wrong for an anatomy chart
# and right for this drawing: the whole pose is about the hands, and they are
# drawn the size the pose needs them to be. Keep them.
#
# Conventions -- angles, `facing`, why shoulders and hips are bones -- are
# written out in full once, in data/characters/test/rig.odrig. The short of it:
# 0 = right, +90 = DOWN, -90 = up, angles are LOCAL to the parent, and `facing`
# is the COSINE of the hand's roll, so +-1 is square to the viewer and 0 is
# edge-on.

# ── WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE ──
#
# Colours read off the drawing. This is not art and does not pretend to be --
# RigDraw::drawSkin builds a coat, a hat and a face out of the bones and the
# cloth chains, and these nine numbers are everything it is told about the
# character's appearance.
#
# The coat is the two cloth chains with the space between them filled, so it
# flaps because the solver made it flap: `drag`, `wind` and the rest of the
# fabric numbers below reach the picture without the renderer knowing they
# exist.
#
# Remove this line and the character falls back to the debug skeleton, which is
# still the right view for asking where an elbow is.
look coat #5d6c80 lapel #39434f shirt #c9d2da skin #8d9aa8 hat #26262c band #c9ccd2 line #14141a wearshat 1 brim 1.6

# ── Spine. Long torso, short neck: the head sits down into the coat collar. ──
bone root    len 0   angle 0
bone hips    parent root  len 0   angle 0    sway 1.0
bone chest   parent hips  len 76  angle -90  breath 1.0  sway 0.5  collide 26
bone neck    parent chest len 12  angle 0    breath 0.4  gaze 0.3
head head    parent neck  len 22  angle 0    size 46     gaze 1.0  breath 0.2  mouth -0.45
#            ^ the resting mouth: down at the corners. This one has been
#              awake too long, and a pose has to work to change it.

# ── Pelvis and legs. Scissored off at the knee on stage; they are here so the
#    sway has something to move and the stance reads as weight on one foot. ──
bone hipL    parent hips len 15 angle 180  sway 0.6  collide 22
bone hipR    parent hips len 15 angle 0    sway 0.6  collide 22
bone legL    parent hipL len 44 angle -90  collide 15
bone legR    parent hipR len 44 angle 90  collide 15

# ── Shoulders. The measured span, and the thing that makes this a coat. ──
bone clavL   parent chest len 58 angle 90   breath 0.6
bone armL.up parent clavL len 51 angle 90   breath 0.2
bone armL.fore parent armL.up len 46 angle 10
hand handL   parent armL.fore len 0 angle 0 atlas hands size 58

bone clavR   parent chest len 58 angle -90  breath 0.6
bone armR.up parent clavR len 51 angle -90  breath 0.2
bone armR.fore parent armR.up len 46 angle -10
hand handR   parent armR.fore len 0 angle 0 atlas hands size 58

# ── The overcoat: long, heavy, open at the front. ──
#
# Hung off the clavicles, which now reach the real shoulder, so it falls from
# where a coat actually falls rather than from beside the neck. Two and a bit
# head-diameters of it, which puts the hem below the stage crop -- correct for
# a coat that keeps going past the bottom of the shot.
#
#   gravity 540  wool, not a suit
#   damp 0.94    heavy cloth keeps its swing
#   stiff 0.55   soft enough to fold rather than swing as a board
#   drag 1.4     mass gets LEFT BEHIND by the shoulder and cracks at the hem
#                when the movement stops. See Cloth::drag.
#   wind 0.65    and catches less of a gust than a lighter garment, which is
#                the whole reason windCatch is per-garment
# `pair` and `width` are what make these two chains ONE COAT rather than two
# curtains: joined below the armhole, and joined at 82 units where the shoulders
# are 116 apart, so the cloth comes in under the arms and swings out again over
# the hips. `collide` on the body above is the other half of it -- the panels
# ride over the hips and get carried along by a leg instead of hanging through
# both.
cloth coatL parent clavL segs 10 len 15 stiff 0.55 damp 0.94 gravity 540 drag 1.4 wind 0.65 pair coatR width 82
cloth coatR parent clavR segs 10 len 15 stiff 0.55 damp 0.94 gravity 540 drag 1.4 wind 0.65 pair coatL width 82

part head         file art/head.png     pivot 0.500,0.812 turn 90  unit 4
part chest        file art/coat.png     pivot 0.500,0.621 turn 90  unit 4
part armL.up      file art/armup.png    pivot 0.169,0.500 turn 0   unit 4
part armR.up      file art/armup.png    pivot 0.169,0.500 turn 0   unit 4
part armL.fore    file art/armfore.png  pivot 0.171,0.500 turn 0   unit 4
part armR.fore    file art/armfore.png  pivot 0.171,0.500 turn 0   unit 4

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Poses. Named to match the rest of the cast wherever they mean the same
# thing, so a script can change who is speaking without changing what they are
# doing. A pose says only what it CHANGES; anything it does not name returns
# to rest, so poses cannot quietly compose.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# THE DRAWING, and the reason this rig exists. The tutorial asks for this
# pose by name on the page where the fleet is sighted.
#
# Every angle here is read off it rather than invented:
#
#   upper arm   world  155 deg  -- out to the side and DOWN a little
#   forearm     world  -82 deg  -- back up, very slightly outward
#
# which is a 57-degree elbow: sharply bent, elbow low and wide, hands level
# with the top of the hat. Written as locals against the parent's world angle,
# which is what makes the signs look strange -- the forearm's local is +123 on
# the arm whose upper arm is -31, because THE ELBOW BREAKS OUTWARD. Raising
# your arms with the palms turned to somebody rotates the whole arm at the
# shoulder and the elbow goes with it. Bend them the same way and the arm comes
# out nearly straight and the hands read as stuck on the ends.
#
# facing 1.0 is deliberate: it is the only value at which the palm is at full
# width, and it is what the drawing shows. Backing off "for some depth" turns
# the thumb back across the palm and shortens it.
pose hands_up 0.55s inoutquad
  head       mouth -0.2 jaw 0.35
  chest      -90
  neck       -3
  clavL      84
  clavR      -84
  armL.up    31
  armL.fore  -123
  armR.up    -31
  armR.fore  123
  handL      open facing 1.0
  handR      open facing 1.0

# Arms down, weight settled, backs of the hands to the viewer. What everything
# returns to.
pose idle 0.5s inoutquad
  chest      -89
  neck       4
  clavL      86
  clavR      -86
  armL.up    82
  armL.fore  12
  armR.up    -82
  armR.fore  -12
  handL      relaxed facing -0.85
  handR      relaxed facing -0.85

# Hands spread low and open -- "what do you want me to do about it". The same
# palms as hands_up, at waist height, which is the cheap half of the gesture
# and reads completely differently.
#
# The elbow carries most of the bend rather than the shoulder. On arms this
# long -- 97 units, against the Cryptographer's 62 -- an arm opened at the
# shoulder puts the hand somewhere off the side of the stage, and the gesture
# stops being a shrug of the hands and becomes a wingspan.
pose spread 0.45s inoutquad
  chest      -88
  neck       6
  head       4 mouth -0.15 jaw 0.5
  clavL      92
  clavR      -92
  armL.up    74
  armL.fore  -44
  armR.up    -74
  armR.fore  44
  handL      open facing 0.8
  handR      open facing 0.8

# One hand up to the brim. The other stays down.
pose tip_hat 0.5s inoutquad
  head       mouth -0.1
  chest      -90
  neck       -2
  clavL      86
  clavR      -80
  armL.up    82
  armL.fore  12
  armR.up    -128
  armR.fore  -104
  handL      relaxed facing -0.85
  handR      relaxed facing -0.3

# Pointing something out. The elbow keeps a bend in it: an arm run out straight
# is a signpost, and the difference is about twenty degrees at the elbow.
pose point 0.4s inoutquad
  chest      -92
  neck       -8
  head       -4
  clavL      88
  clavR      -86
  armL.up    80
  armL.fore  12
  armR.up    -44
  armR.fore  36
  handL      relaxed facing -0.85
  handR      point facing -0.85

# One hand to the chin. Required by any script that reuses the Advisor's pages.
pose think 0.6s inoutquad
  chest      -87
  neck       11
  head       7 mouth -0.75
  clavL      88
  clavR      -80
  armL.up    80
  armL.fore  88
  armR.up    -142
  armR.fore  -122
  handL      relaxed facing -0.7
  handR      fist facing -0.6

# A shrug is a MOVEMENT, not a posture: the shoulders go up fast, are seen up,
# and come back down. Which is why this is the pose with keyframes on it, and
# why the way in is `outback` -- an "out" easing leaves at full speed, which is
# wrong for an arm swing and exactly right for a shrug.
pose shrug 0.24s outback hold 0.4s then idle
  head       mouth -0.6
  chest      -86
  neck       6
  clavL      58
  clavR      -58
  armL.up    54
  armL.fore  -36
  armR.up    -54
  armR.fore  36
  handL      open facing 0.85
  handR      open facing 0.85

# ...and down again. A keyframe inherits everything it does not name, so the
# arms stay out here while the shoulders drop and the hands fall, and
# `then idle` puts the body away after a beat rather than leaving it standing
# with its arms out for the rest of the scene.
key 0.5s inoutquad hold 1.0s
  clavL      80
  clavR      -80
  armL.up    62
  armL.fore  28
  armR.up    -62
  armR.fore  -28
